1
00:00:00,520 --> 00:00:06,940
Unrepresentative Democracy Princeton proved that public opinion has zero effect on U.S.

2
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:14,540
policy. Corporations spend four point four billion dollars on lobbying and get an eighteen to one return.

3
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:21,060
Your vote is worth zero point zero zero zero one seven dollars.

4
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:25,120
Here's how to make the math work for medicine instead of missiles.

5
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:30,440
Your chance of deciding a presidential election: one in sixty million.

6
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:34,480
You're more likely to die in a car crash driving to the polls.

7
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:40,700
The expected value of your vote is zero point zero zero zero one seven dollars.

8
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:46,420
The cost of casting it (time, gas, parking) is about fifty dollars.

9
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:52,700
Democracy was designed so that your individual participation is mathematically meaningless.

10
00:00:53,180 --> 00:01:00,800
What isn't meaningless: the four point four billion dollars corporations spend annually on lobbying, according to Open Secrets

11
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:07,560
twenty twenty-four. That math works out just fine. The Founding Fathers were brilliant men.

12
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:13,620
They anticipated tyranny, corruption, foreign invasion, and mob rule.

13
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:20,840
They did not anticipate Florida. What Democracy Should Look Like versus Reality.

14
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:28,200
Princeton University analyzed one thousand seven hundred and seventy-nine policy decisions over twenty years.

15
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:33,170
The correlation between what ordinary voters want and what actually happens?

16
00:01:33,820 --> 00:01:39,240
Zero. Percent. When economic elites want something? seventy-eight percent of the time.

17
00:01:40,140 --> 00:01:46,140
You have the same political influence as your houseplant. Slightly less, actually.

18
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:50,200
Your houseplant produces oxygen, which at least one congressman needs.

19
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:58,240
Your institutions tell the whole story in their names. You have a "Department of Defense" that mainly attacks people.

20
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:03,320
A "Department of Health" that has not yet produced any observable health.

21
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:07,780
And a "Department of Justice" that (you can see where this is going).

22
00:02:08,639 --> 00:02:15,770
Whether zero percent or one hundred percent of voters support a bill, it has the same roughly thirty percent chance of passing.

23
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:22,820
Democracy is a slot machine where every pull gives the same result but you keep playing because the machine has an American

24
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:30,440
flag on it. The Mathematics of Political Failure. Concentrated Benefits versus Diffuse Costs.

25
00:02:31,580 --> 00:02:36,500
Sugar subsidies cost Americans three point five billion dollars per year.

26
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:41,640
That's ten dollars per person. Will you spend weeks fighting to save ten dollars?

27
00:02:42,220 --> 00:02:47,680
No. Will sugar companies spend millions to keep their three point five billion dollars?

28
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:56,560
Yes. Guess who wins? This pattern repeats everywhere: Pharma companies see five hundred billion dollars in concentrated

29
00:02:56,560 --> 00:03:02,500
profits. Patients suffer one thousand five hundred dollars each in diffuse suffering.

30
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:12,220
The winner is not you. Banks in two thousand and eight received a seven hundred billion dollar concentrated bailout.

31
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:15,780
You paid two thousand one hundred dollars in diffuse taxes.

32
00:03:16,260 --> 00:03:23,180
The winner is the ones with yachts. The Death Spiral: How Two Parties Kill Crowd Wisdom.

33
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:32,140
James Surowiecki demonstrated that crowds outperform experts under four conditions: diversity of opinion, independence of

34
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:36,540
judgment, decentralization, and a good aggregation mechanism.

35
00:03:37,340 --> 00:03:42,920
Your democracy has three of these. It systematically destroys the most important one.

36
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:49,560
Independence means each person decides based on their own knowledge and values, not by watching what everyone else is doing.

37
00:03:50,180 --> 00:03:57,880
First-past-the-post voting annihilates this. You have three hundred and thirty million people, many of whom would make excellent

38
00:03:57,880 --> 00:04:06,250
leaders. But you don't choose among them. You choose between two candidates pre-selected by a process optimized for fundraising,

39
00:04:06,700 --> 00:04:14,210
name recognition, and not offending the party base. Then, even among those two, you don't vote for the one you prefer.

40
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:22,120
You vote against the one you fear. Voters who genuinely prefer a third-party candidate vote for a major-party candidate

41
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:29,280
they dislike to avoid "wasting" their vote. This is called strategic voting, and it means the ballot doesn't measure what

42
00:04:29,280 --> 00:04:34,110
you want. It measures what you think other people will do. That's not independence.

43
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:39,660
That's a herd. Army ants have a behavior called a death spiral.

44
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:47,160
Army ants are nearly blind. They navigate entirely by following pheromone trails laid by the ants ahead of them.

45
00:04:47,840 --> 00:04:54,260
When a foraging column gets separated from the main trail, the leading ants have nothing to follow, so they wander.

46
00:04:54,680 --> 00:05:01,180
But they're still laying pheromone as they walk. If the front of the column curves enough to cross its own trail (or the

47
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:04,940
back of the column), the leaders pick up that pheromone and follow it.

48
00:05:05,480 --> 00:05:12,020
The circle closes. Now every ant is both following and reinforcing the chemical trail that traps it.

49
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:17,920
They march in a loop, sometimes hundreds of thousands strong, until they die of exhaustion.

50
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:25,840
No individual ant is stupid. Each is following a perfectly rational rule: follow the strongest pheromone signal.

51
00:05:26,280 --> 00:05:34,480
The system is stupid. The circle emerges not from bad individuals but from a structure where no ant can navigate independently.

52
00:05:35,440 --> 00:05:39,500
Your two-party system is a death spiral with "I Voted" stickers.

53
00:05:40,120 --> 00:05:44,300
Each voter follows the signal of viability rather than their own judgment.

54
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:50,760
Pollsters tell you who's "electable." Media covers the two "real" candidates.

55
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:58,380
Donors fund the two "viable" options. You vote for one of them because everyone else is voting for one of them because everyone

56
00:05:58,380 --> 00:06:04,940
else is voting for one of them. The best candidate out of three hundred and thirty million people could be standing right

57
00:06:04,940 --> 00:06:12,400
there, but if they're not wearing the right color tie, they're "throwing their vote away," which is a phrase that only makes

58
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:20,740
sense in a system designed to waste votes. The result: a nation of three hundred and thirty million people, selecting from

59
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:27,880
a talent pool of three hundred and thirty million people, reliably produces a choice between two people that approximately

60
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:34,700
nobody is enthusiastic about. This is the ant death spiral with a larger circle and better catering.

61
00:06:35,860 --> 00:06:39,280
Each ant follows the one ahead. The leader follows the last.

62
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:47,360
They march in a perfect circle until they die. Your two-party system works the same way, but the circle takes four years.

63
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:53,660
How Money Buys Power. Campaign contributions don't directly buy how politicians vote.

64
00:06:54,220 --> 00:06:59,640
Only one in four studies support that. The system is much more elegant than bribery.

65
00:07:00,300 --> 00:07:04,420
Bribery is what poor countries do. Rich countries have "campaign finance.".

66
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:11,100
But they do something far more effective: they buy which politicians exist in the first place.

67
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:18,060
Ninety-five percent of House races since two thousand and four were won by the highest spender.

68
00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:24,420
The average winning House campaign costs two point seven nine million dollars.

69
00:07:24,840 --> 00:07:30,880
The average winning Senate campaign costs twenty-six point five three million dollars.

70
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:36,020
Why bribe a politician when you can just manufacture one who already agrees with you?

71
00:07:36,620 --> 00:07:39,120
It's like complaining about the menu when you own the restaurant.

72
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:47,710
For the ones already in office, contributions buy something more subtle than votes: Access means your call gets returned,

73
00:07:47,980 --> 00:07:54,560
while theirs goes to voicemail. Agenda control means your issue gets a hearing, while theirs gets buried.

74
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:58,760
Legislative language provides the exact wording that creates loopholes.

75
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:07,180
Committee positions can be bought, as four hundred and fifty thousand dollars to party committees buys a seat where bills

76
00:08:07,180 --> 00:08:14,300
live or die. You don't buy the vote. You buy the voter, the agenda, and the room where the vote happens.

77
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:25,140
The Lobbying R.O.I.: eighteen to one Returns. The most profitable investment in America isn't stocks, real estate, or crypto.

78
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:32,080
It's congressmen: Disease advocacy groups spend one hundred million dollars on lobbying.

79
00:08:32,580 --> 00:08:36,500
They get one point eight billion dollars in increased funding.

80
00:08:36,940 --> 00:08:43,559
The R.O.I. is an eighteen to one ratio (one thousand seven hundred percent net return).

81
00:08:44,360 --> 00:08:48,970
Meanwhile: Breast cancer has great lobbyists, which leads to massive funding.

82
00:08:49,580 --> 00:08:55,320
Heart disease kills more people, but because they have worse lobbyists, they receive less funding.

83
00:08:56,120 --> 00:09:00,560
Rare pediatric diseases have no lobbyists and therefore get no funding.

84
00:09:01,440 --> 00:09:07,500
Democracy doesn't allocate resources by who's dying. It allocates by who can afford the best lobbyist.

85
00:09:08,280 --> 00:09:12,290
If your disease can't hire a P.R. firm, it's not a real disease.

86
00:09:12,980 --> 00:09:18,860
It's just dying. Your Congressman: A Fundraiser Who Occasionally Legislates.

87
00:09:19,640 --> 00:09:25,880
Congressmen spend four hours a day fundraising. They spend zero hours a day reading bills.

88
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:31,080
Actual governing is squeezed into whatever's left. They don't read what they vote on.

89
00:09:31,620 --> 00:09:38,300
The PATRIOT Act was three hundred and forty-two pages, voted on forty-five minutes after release.

90
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:43,620
That's seven point six pages per minute. Olympic speed-readers can't do that.

91
00:09:44,180 --> 00:09:50,400
The Affordable Care Act was two thousand seven hundred pages; "we have to pass it to see what's in it".

92
00:09:51,400 --> 00:09:57,260
These people control nuclear weapons. Sleep tight. Congressional Committees: The Menu.

93
00:09:57,880 --> 00:10:05,340
Committees control which bills live or die. Both parties assign fundraising quotas for each seat: raise this much for the

94
00:10:05,340 --> 00:10:12,780
party, or lose your assignment. Don't hit your number? Your bills die, your amendments vanish, and your name goes on a literal

95
00:10:12,780 --> 00:10:17,680
"wall of shame". It's like a restaurant where the waiters have to pay for their own sections.

96
00:10:18,840 --> 00:10:25,000
Here's the menu. Want to be House Speaker? Raise twenty to thirty-one million dollars for your party.

97
00:10:25,420 --> 00:10:29,310
Want to chair a powerful committee like Ways and Means or Appropriations?

98
00:10:29,820 --> 00:10:34,360
That'll cost you about one point two to one point eight million dollars in party fundraising.

99
00:10:34,980 --> 00:10:41,860
Even a basic freshman member owes a six-figure fundraising quota just to keep their seat at the table.

100
00:10:42,460 --> 00:10:48,260
The House Speaker position costs thirty-one million dollars for the D.C.C.C.

101
00:10:48,260 --> 00:10:54,260
and twenty million dollars for the N.R.C.C. because that person controls the entire agenda.

102
00:10:55,140 --> 00:10:59,800
The Ways and Means Chair costs one point eight million dollars for the D.C.C.C.

103
00:11:00,080 --> 00:11:05,720
and one point two million dollars for the N.R.C.C. because all tax laws start here.

104
00:11:06,620 --> 00:11:11,120
The Appropriations Chair costs one point eight million dollars for the D.C.C.C.

105
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:18,580
and one point two million dollars for the N.R.C.C. because that role controls a one point seven trillion dollar budget.

106
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:24,140
The Energy and Commerce Chair costs one point eight million dollars for the D.C.C.C.

107
00:11:24,420 --> 00:11:30,780
and one point two million dollars for the N.R.C.C. because they oversee healthcare, energy, and telecom.

108
00:11:31,580 --> 00:11:38,020
A Regular Committee seat costs between five hundred thousand and one million dollars for the D.C.C.C.

109
00:11:38,020 --> 00:11:43,740
and eight hundred and seventy-five thousand dollars for the N.R.C.C., which still beats irrelevance.

110
00:11:44,660 --> 00:11:51,600
A Basic Member pays one hundred and fifty thousand dollars or more for the D.C.C.C., while the N.R.C.C.

111
00:11:51,680 --> 00:11:58,660
cost varies as the entry fee to play. This is why congressmen spend four hours a day fundraising.

112
00:11:59,140 --> 00:12:06,840
They're not campaigning. They're paying rent. The people writing your healthcare laws literally bought their positions from

113
00:12:06,840 --> 00:12:12,620
the industries those laws regulate. On your planet, this is called "the system working.".

114
00:12:14,320 --> 00:12:22,620
Public Choice Theory: The Nobel Prize for Cynicism. In nineteen eighty-six, James Buchanan won the Nobel Prize in Economics

115
00:12:22,820 --> 00:12:27,360
for formally proving what anyone who has ever been to the D.M.V.

116
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:32,920
already knew: People don't become angels when you give them a government I.D.

117
00:12:33,020 --> 00:12:41,280
badge. This is Public Choice Theory: bureaucrats, politicians, and regulators are JUST AS SELFISH as everyone else.

118
00:12:41,860 --> 00:12:46,800
The only difference is that when a corporation is greedy, you can stop buying their product.

119
00:12:47,300 --> 00:12:51,540
When a bureaucracy is greedy, they can arrest you for not buying their product.

120
00:12:52,520 --> 00:13:02,040
What They Actually Maximize. The N.I.H. should maximize cures, but it actually maximizes grants awarded, resulting in no

121
00:13:02,040 --> 00:13:11,580
cures. The F.D.A. should maximize lives saved, but it actually maximizes avoiding lawsuits, resulting in deadly delays.

122
00:13:12,280 --> 00:13:20,000
Congress should maximize voter welfare, but it actually maximizes campaign donations, resulting in corporate welfare.

123
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:28,880
Pharma should maximize health outcomes, but it actually maximizes recurring revenue, meaning chronic disease is preferred.

124
00:13:29,620 --> 00:13:37,540
A Defense Contractor should maximize national security, but it actually maximizes government contracts, resulting in endless

125
00:13:37,540 --> 00:13:42,420
wars. Nobody in the system is incentivized to cure disease.

126
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:50,400
Or end war. You could elect five hundred and thirty-five clones of Gandhi and within two years they'd be taking pharma money.

127
00:13:50,940 --> 00:13:57,060
The system digests idealists like a stomach digests food. It doesn't matter what you put in.

128
00:13:57,440 --> 00:14:06,420
You get the same output. The Libertarian Paradox. Libertarians say: Government fails because government is government.

129
00:14:07,060 --> 00:14:11,120
Socialists say: Government fails because it needs more government.

130
00:14:11,960 --> 00:14:18,700
Public Choice Theory says: Government fails because the people running it are people, and people are terrible.

131
00:14:19,500 --> 00:14:29,200
The solution: Stop expecting people to be good. Design systems where being selfish accidentally produces good outcomes.

132
00:14:29,640 --> 00:14:37,480
Like capitalism, but for not dying. Regulatory Capture: How Industries Write Their Own Rules.

133
00:14:37,920 --> 00:14:42,020
There are thirteen thousand seven hundred registered lobbyists in Washington.

134
00:14:42,520 --> 00:14:48,340
Half of them used to work in the government they're now bribing, which on your planet is called "a career path" rather than

135
00:14:48,340 --> 00:14:54,400
"corruption." Corporations spend four point four billion dollars a year on lobbying.

136
00:14:55,020 --> 00:15:01,880
Divide that by five hundred and thirty-five members of Congress and you get over eight million dollars in professional persuasion

137
00:15:01,880 --> 00:15:06,380
surrounding each congressman who earns less than a mid-level dentist.

138
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:11,180
The mystery is not that they're corrupt. The mystery is that any of them aren't.

139
00:15:12,240 --> 00:15:17,920
Citizens United made corporations people who can donate unlimited money through Super PACs.

140
00:15:18,440 --> 00:15:25,500
Corporations are people now, which is odd because you can't imprison a corporation, give it chemotherapy, or make it feel

141
00:15:25,500 --> 00:15:28,940
shame. Pharmaceutical companies write their own regulations.

142
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:34,040
The Medicare Modernization Act banned the government from negotiating drug prices.

143
00:15:34,800 --> 00:15:39,920
That's why insulin that costs six dollars to make costs you three hundred dollars.

144
00:15:40,280 --> 00:15:46,880
A four thousand nine hundred percent markup. Your street-corner drug dealers find this excessive, and they don't even have

145
00:15:46,880 --> 00:15:54,680
shareholders. The military-industrial complex distributes operations across forty-two states so that cutting any program

146
00:15:54,680 --> 00:15:58,900
threatens local jobs in enough congressional districts to make it impossible.

147
00:15:59,480 --> 00:16:03,040
It's a tumor that grew its own blood supply. But the M.I.C.

148
00:16:03,260 --> 00:16:08,290
doesn't just lobby for budgets; it produces the threats that justify them.

149
00:16:08,420 --> 00:16:14,880
Robert Pape at the University of Chicago found that ninety-five percent of suicide terrorist attacks from nineteen eighty

150
00:16:14,880 --> 00:16:21,040
to two thousand and three were driven by foreign military occupation, not ideology.

151
00:16:21,080 --> 00:16:28,760
Half the attackers were secular. Your eight trillion dollar War on Terror took terrorist attacks from roughly one thousand

152
00:16:28,760 --> 00:16:37,540
per year to nearly seventeen thousand by twenty fourteen. Military bases abroad create the resistance that becomes the terrorism

153
00:16:37,540 --> 00:16:41,960
that justifies the military budgets that fund more bases abroad.

154
00:16:42,620 --> 00:16:49,980
This is regulatory capture with an extra step: instead of just writing your own regulations, you create the conditions that

155
00:16:49,980 --> 00:16:55,440
make the regulations seem necessary. Most industries merely buy favorable rules.

156
00:16:55,860 --> 00:17:01,060
The defense industry manufactures the emergencies that make the rules look urgent.

157
00:17:01,840 --> 00:17:08,020
The Pentagon has never passed an audit but somehow knows it needs more money (they can't tell you where the last trillion

158
00:17:08,020 --> 00:17:11,260
went, but they're very confident about needing the next one).

159
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:15,560
Pharma employs three lobbyists for every member of Congress.

160
00:17:16,060 --> 00:17:21,119
The F.D.A. gets seventy-five percent of its drug review budget from the companies it regulates.

161
00:17:21,640 --> 00:17:27,660
For details, see F.D.A.: Unsafe and Ineffective. And the ratchet only turns one way.

162
00:17:28,220 --> 00:17:34,420
After World War Two, the U.S. cut military spending by eighty-seven point six percent in two years.

163
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:38,540
G.M. went from bombers back to Cadillacs. The economy boomed.

164
00:17:39,060 --> 00:17:42,840
But the military-industrial complex made sure that never happened again.

165
00:17:43,660 --> 00:17:52,000
In inflation-adjusted dollars, peacetime spending is now thirty point six times the pre-World War Two baseline: twenty-nine

166
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:59,840
billion dollars in nineteen thirty-nine versus eight hundred and eighty-six billion dollars today, in constant dollars,

167
00:18:00,140 --> 00:18:04,440
with no world war to justify it. The tumor doesn't just grow its own blood supply.

168
00:18:05,220 --> 00:18:12,860
It grows and then reclassifies the growth as vital organs. The system maintains itself with the elegant efficiency of a

169
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:16,580
parasite that keeps its host alive just enough to keep feeding.

170
00:18:17,480 --> 00:18:22,500
Cures are a one-time payment; treatments are recurring revenue.

171
00:18:22,820 --> 00:18:31,160
Approving a bad drug ends careers; delaying a good one ends lives (but not careers, and careers are what matter to people

172
00:18:31,160 --> 00:18:36,080
with careers). Everyone who matters wins. Everyone who's dying loses.

173
00:18:36,680 --> 00:18:41,140
The system works perfectly. It's just optimized for the wrong thing.

174
00:18:42,240 --> 00:18:48,000
The Solution They Don't Want. Lobbying is just one gear in the machine.

175
00:18:48,520 --> 00:18:56,680
The full cost of political dysfunction (military overspend, healthcare administration, regulatory friction, tax complexity,

176
00:18:57,240 --> 00:19:05,300
corporate welfare, and the rest) totals four point nine trillion dollars per year, or seventeen percent of G.D.P.

177
00:19:05,940 --> 00:19:13,780
A forensic audit (the "Political Dysfunction Tax") identifies two point four five trillion dollars in recoverable capital

178
00:19:13,800 --> 00:19:20,360
per year if U.S. governance merely reached O.E.C.D. median efficiency.

179
00:19:20,420 --> 00:19:27,520
You don't need a revolution. You need a reallocation. But nobody fights to recover it, because the losses are diffuse and

180
00:19:27,520 --> 00:19:35,380
the benefits are concentrated. Same pattern as this entire chapter: each corporation gains one hundred million dollars (worth

181
00:19:35,380 --> 00:19:39,760
a thousand phone calls, a lobbyist army, and a senator's vacation home).

182
00:19:40,600 --> 00:19:49,020
Each citizen loses thousands (not worth a phone call). But what if curing disease was more profitable than treating it?

183
00:19:49,680 --> 00:19:54,560
What if peace paid better than war? That's what a one percent treaty does.

184
00:19:55,120 --> 00:19:58,600
It doesn't fight the corruption machine. It feeds it different inputs.

185
00:19:59,240 --> 00:20:05,960
You're going to out-bribe the bribers with math. P.S. - If this chapter depressed you, good.

186
00:20:05,960 --> 00:20:08,680
Depression is the correct response to your political system.

187
00:20:09,200 --> 00:20:16,080
But remember: the same corruption that lets corporations buy politicians also lets you buy them back.

188
00:20:16,840 --> 00:20:23,630
Politicians don't have principles. They have prices. And prices, unlike principles, can be negotiated.
